### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

An outer `@boundary` doesn't catch constructor errors from a deferred component. The chunk loads, the placeholder disappears, and the region stays blank. The error reaches `ErrorHandler.handleError()`; the fallback and Retry button never appear.

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-outer-immediate',
  imports: [Widget],
  template: `
    <section id="region">
      @boundary {
        @defer (on immediate) {
          <app-widget />
        } @placeholder {
          <p class="placeholder">Widget placeholder</p>
        }
      } @error {
        <p class="fallback">Widget unavailable: {{ $error.message }}</p>
        <button id="retry" (click)="$reset()">Retry</button>
      }
    </section>
  `,
})
export class OuterImmediate {}
```

`app-widget` throws in its constructor:

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-widget',
  template: `<p class="ready">Widget ready</p>`,
})
export class Widget {
  constructor() {
    throw new Error('Widget initialization failed');
  }
}
```

I expected the boundary to show the fallback. This also happens with `when`. Throwing in `ngOnInit`, or moving the boundary inside `@defer`, works.

