### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

compiler-cli

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

The `deferTriggerMisconfiguration` extended diagnostic (NG8021) warns that a `prefetch when` trigger "matches the main trigger and provides no benefit" whenever the main trigger is also `when`, even when the two triggers watch **different** expressions.

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [HeavyComponent],
  template: `
    <!-- Render once the panel is opened; start loading as soon as it is requested. -->
    @defer (when isOpen(); prefetch when isRequested()) {
      <heavy-cmp />
    }
  `,
})
export class AppComponent {
  readonly isRequested = signal(false);
  readonly isOpen = signal(false);
}
```

**Expected:** no warning. `isRequested()` can become `true` long before `isOpen()` does, and then the prefetch loads the dependencies early. That is exactly what `prefetch when` is for.

**Actual:** NG8021 is reported on `prefetch when isRequested()` (output below).

The runtime honours the prefetch on its own: `ɵɵdeferPrefetchWhen` binds the prefetch expression's own value and calls `triggerPrefetching` once it is `true` and loading has not started. It does not read the main trigger. In our app, a `prefetch when` like this one makes the deferred chunk load in parallel with the request the main condition waits for, instead of after it.

**Cause:** in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/extended/checks/defer_trigger_misconfiguration/index.ts`, timer/idle pairs compare their timings and hover/interaction/viewport pairs compare their references and options. A `when` pair (`TmplAstBoundDeferredTrigger`) has no branch of its own, though. It falls through to the generic "syntactic identical" branch:

```ts
if (
  main.constructor === pre.constructor &&
  !(main instanceof TmplAstTimerDeferredTrigger)
) {
```

That branch reports any two triggers of the same class and never compares their `value` expressions. The message prints `'bound'` because the kind name is derived from `BoundDeferredTrigger`.

#67497 fixed the same false positive for `idle` pairs with different timeouts; `when` is the remaining case.

**Suggested fix:** handle `when` pairs like the reference-based triggers: warn only when the prefetch expression is the same as the main one (e.g. `when ready(); prefetch when ready()`), and stay silent when they differ. I'm happy to send a PR.

**Workarounds** all have a cost: suppressing `deferTriggerMisconfiguration` silences its genuine reports in every template; `prefetch on idle` fetches the chunk on every visit; dropping the prefetch delays the chunk.

